# tools/c/convergence_tree_prove_witness.rish -- the writers no single-file prover could reach.
#
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/c/convergence_tree_prove_witness.rish
#
# `tools/c/convergence_prove.sh` settles convergence by RUNNING, and it invokes a tool as
# `<tool> <one-path>`. Measured `20260908.190452`, NINE of the ten tools the census finds answer
# to a flag rather than a path -- `apply`, `write`, `--check` -- because they are whole-tree
# operators that find their own work. So the census column read whether somebody WROTE an
# idempotence check, and for nine of ten nobody could have run one. This proves the instrument that
# can: the subject is a repository, the pen is a `git worktree` checkout of HEAD, and the tree under
# test is never touched.
#
# TWO LEGS PROVE THE TWO CHOICES THAT ARE NEW, rather than only the verdicts.
#
# THE IGNORED-PATH LEG (`20260909.201720`). The comparison is `git write-tree`, which stages nothing
# `.gitignore` denies -- and this tree denies its whole repository root, so a write there moved the
# disk and moved no hash. An operator appending to an ignored `notes.txt` on every run read `inert`,
# and a perturbation writing an ignored root file read `perturb_inert`, whose own words are "the
# sample never landed". `unseen` and `perturb_unseen` refuse instead, and the control shows one
# operator across two pen repositories differing only in their `.gitignore`.
#
# THE MODE LEG. An operator toggling a file's exec bit changes no LINE, so a prover comparing diffs
# would call it converged. The comparison here is `git write-tree` -- one hash over every path, its
# bytes and its mode -- so the toggle reads `diverges`. `.claude/rules/exec-bit.md` is thirty-nine
# lost exec bits' worth of argument that a mode is content the repository tracks.
#
# A SIXTH VERDICT THE WITNESS ITSELF EARNED. The first version of the assertion below passed a
# `sed` expression through Rishi, whose escaping doubled the backslashes, so the expression matched
# nothing and exited zero. The prover read `inert` and this witness refused -- correctly, and for
# the wrong reason, since nothing was wrong with the tool. `perturb_inert` now tells a sample that
# never landed apart from a tool with no work, by asking the tree rather than the exit status.
#
# THE PERTURBATION WRITES THROUGH THE ORIGINAL INODE rather than reaching for an in-place flag.
# GNU and BSD spell `sed -i` differently and `shell_dialect` gates it at zero, so the portable form
# this tree already prescribes -- write a temporary, `cat` it back -- is what a pen gets too.
#
# A SECOND SUBJECT, AND A SHAPE NEITHER PROVER'S USAGE NAMED (`20260909.201050`). The paragraph
# above counts the nine that answer to a FLAG, and `tools/fixtures/r/readme_metrics_splice.sh`
# answers to neither a flag nor a single path: it takes TWO -- the target file and the file holding
# the rendered block. So the sibling prover cannot reach it either. `tools/c/convergence_prove.sh`
# invokes a tool as `sh <tool> <one-path>`, and handed one argument the splice exits at its own
# `${2:?}` guard. Measured on metal:
# `sh tools/c/convergence_prove.sh tools/fixtures/r/readme_metrics_splice.sh README.md` answers
# `verdict=refused -- the first run exited non-zero`, naming the missing block file. The variadic
# argument list here reached it with no change to this prover at all, which is the honest finding:
# the instrument was already able, and nobody had asked it. `tools/hooks/pre-commit` runs that
# splice on every commit this tree makes, so it is the second-busiest writing in the tree after the
# headline writer below.
#
# THE PERTURBATION IS ANCHORED ON THE MARKER, NEVER ON A ROW OF THE BLOCK. The block's rows are
# generated metrics and their wording is free to change with the tree, where the begin marker is
# the one thing the splice's own contract requires to exist -- it refuses outright without it. So
# the sample drops the line AFTER the marker rather than a named row, and it lands whatever the
# block currently says. A sample that stopped landing would read `perturb_inert` rather than
# blaming the tool, which is the whole reason that verdict exists.
#
# AND THE BLOCK FILE KEEPS THE MARKERS, which is a property of the sample rather than of the tool.
# The splice replaces marker to marker INCLUSIVE, so a block captured without them destroys both,
# the second run refuses `no begin marker in README.md`, and the prover reads `write_once` -- a
# true verdict about a sample that asked the wrong question. The `sed -n` range below captures the
# markers with the body.
#
# THE PERTURB LEG, and it is the honest half. For a whole-tree operator the sample IS the tree, and
# a committed tree usually already holds the state the operator produces -- `reds_ledger_headline_write.sh`
# runs in `tools/hooks/pre-commit` on every commit, so at HEAD it has nothing left to do and reads
# `inert`. That reading is true and proves nothing. `--perturb` hands the pen a tree with work
# waiting, exactly as the sibling prover takes a sample file, and the control shows one operator on
# one repository reading `inert` and then `converges` with only the perturbation between them.

say "convergence-tree-prove: Language EN -- a prover whose subject is a repository."
say "convergence-tree-prove: Style Gauge, Meter -- nine verdicts, and the two that refuse to guess are named."
say "convergence-tree-prove: Lens TAME -- safety first: the pen is a throwaway checkout, never the tree."

say "convergence-tree-prove: the control -- forty-two behaviors, including an operator whose whole output git ignores..."
let ctl = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/c/convergence_tree_prove_control.sh"]
assert ctl.ok else "convergence-tree-prove: the control refused --\n${ctl.out}\n${ctl.err}"
assert ctl.out contains "pass=42 fail=0" else "convergence-tree-prove: the control did not prove forty-two behaviors --\n${ctl.out}\n${ctl.err}"

# invariant: the control exercises a mode-only divergence, both refusal shapes, and the perturb
# pair -- each of which a passing-direction-only proof would miss. A refusal proven only in the
# passing direction cannot be told from a bypass.
assert ctl.out contains "coverage: mode divergence, the perturb pair, a no-op perturbation, an ignored-root write and an ignored-root perturbation shown from both sides, both refusal shapes, an in-tree operator rooted at $0, a Rishi operator against a shell-only prover, and the elder prover's own leak were each exercised" else "convergence-tree-prove: the control never exercised its own teeth"

# invariant: the prover answers for the busiest writing this tree does. `reds_ledger_headline_write.sh`
# runs on every commit through `tools/hooks/pre-commit`, and it is one of the nine no prover could
# reach. On a tree whose headline has been perturbed it has real work, and it converges.
say "convergence-tree-prove: the headline writer that runs on every commit, on a tree with work waiting..."
let hw = run ["sh" "tools/c/convergence_tree_prove.sh" "--perturb" "sed 's/^..Rows: [0-9][0-9]*/**Rows: 1/' construction/REDS.md > .perturbed && cat .perturbed > construction/REDS.md && rm -f .perturbed" "tools/fixtures/r/reds_ledger_headline_write.sh" "write"]
assert hw.ok else "convergence-tree-prove: the headline writer did not converge --\n${hw.out}\n${hw.err}"
assert hw.out contains "verdict=converges" else "convergence-tree-prove: the headline writer read something other than converges --\n${hw.out}"

# THE TWO PAGES EVERY COMMIT REGENERATES THAT NO PROVER COULD REACH. `tools/hooks/pre-commit`
# rewrites three pages on every commit this tree makes, and the leg above proves ONE of them. The
# other two are Rishi operators, and every invocation in both provers read `sh <tool>` -- so a
# Rishi subject was parsed as shell and answered `verdict=refused` over a syntax error, which reads
# as the tool refusing. The dispatch that repairs it is one `case` on the subject's suffix, and
# these are the subjects it was owed.
say "convergence-tree-prove: the front door's metrics block, a Rishi operator, on a perturbed tree..."
let rm2 = run ["sh" "tools/c/convergence_tree_prove.sh" "--perturb" "sed 's/^| Reading | Now |/| Reading | Then |/' README.md > .perturbed && cat .perturbed > README.md && rm -f .perturbed" "tools/r/readme_metrics.rish" "write"]
assert rm2.ok else "convergence-tree-prove: the metrics writer did not converge --\n${rm2.out}\n${rm2.err}"
assert rm2.out contains "verdict=converges" else "convergence-tree-prove: the metrics writer read something other than converges --\n${rm2.out}"

say "convergence-tree-prove: the crushed library index, the second Rishi operator..."
let gl = run ["sh" "tools/c/convergence_tree_prove.sh" "--perturb" "sed 's/^# The Libraries/# The Library/' docs-geode/libraries/README.md > .perturbed && cat .perturbed > docs-geode/libraries/README.md && rm -f .perturbed" "tools/g/geode_libraries.rish" "write"]
assert gl.ok else "convergence-tree-prove: the library index writer did not converge --\n${gl.out}\n${gl.err}"
assert gl.out contains "verdict=converges" else "convergence-tree-prove: the library index writer read something other than converges --\n${gl.out}"

# invariant: the prover answers for a two-path writer neither prover's usage names, and the tree
# it leaves after one run is the tree it leaves after two. `tools/hooks/pre-commit` runs this
# splice on every commit.
say "convergence-tree-prove: the README metrics splice, which takes two paths and no flag..."
let sp = run ["sh" "tools/c/convergence_tree_prove.sh" "--perturb" "sed -n '/^<!-- metrics:begin/,/^<!-- metrics:end -->/p' README.md > .metrics-block && sed '/^<!-- metrics:begin/{n;d;}' README.md > .p && cat .p > README.md && rm -f .p" "tools/fixtures/r/readme_metrics_splice.sh" "README.md" ".metrics-block"]
assert sp.ok else "convergence-tree-prove: the metrics splice did not converge --\n${sp.out}\n${sp.err}"
assert sp.out contains "verdict=converges" else "convergence-tree-prove: the metrics splice read something other than converges --\n${sp.out}"

# THE FIRST SUBJECT THE COMMITTED TREE ALREADY HAS WORK FOR, and it is the one that makes the
# perturb leg above legible by contrast. Every subject before this one runs in `tools/hooks/pre-commit`,
# so at HEAD its work is already done and a bare reading answers `inert` -- which is why each of
# them is handed a perturbation. `tools/fixtures/r/rish_spoken_ascii_convert.sh` is a SWEEP rather
# than a regenerator: read `20260910.211856` over 2,485 tracked `.rish` sources, 1,501 of them still
# hold a character it would convert, so the committed tree is its own sample and no perturbation is
# owed.
#
# THE SUBJECT IS CHOSEN FOR ITS HELD LINES. `tools/m/mycelium_chorus_knot_witness.rish` carries
# convertible spoken text AND two COUPLED sayings -- lines another runner greps for, which the
# converter holds back whole. A converter that converts some lines of a file and refuses others is
# where a second run is most likely to move: the hold-back set is derived on every run rather than
# pinned, so a first run that changed the file could hand the second run a different derivation. It
# does not, and this leg is what says so.
#
# AND THE FIRST SUBJECT THIS LEG REACHED FOR WAS PUT BACK, which is worth writing down rather than
# quietly fixing. `tools/gen/chapter/prin_scope.rish` holds three coupled sayings and was the better
# sample; naming it here moved it out of `unheard_guard`'s `unnamed_choirs` set -- 11 to 10 -- and
# reddened that guard's published leg. The fall was bought by a MENTION. This leg hands prin_scope
# to a converter as a data file; it never RUNS it, and `unheard_guard`'s own first line is *a guard
# that is never run guards nothing*. So the subject moved to a file that is not a silent choir, and
# the reading that guard publishes stays honest. The blind spot it names -- a path cited as data
# reading as a path that was run -- is that guard's lane rather than this one's.
say "convergence-tree-prove: the Rishi spoken-ASCII sweep, on a file whose held lines it must re-derive..."
let rs = run ["sh" "tools/c/convergence_tree_prove.sh" "tools/fixtures/r/rish_spoken_ascii_convert.sh" "--apply" "tools/m/mycelium_chorus_knot_witness.rish"]
assert rs.ok else "convergence-tree-prove: the Rishi spoken sweep did not converge --\n${rs.out}\n${rs.err}"
assert rs.out contains "verdict=converges" else "convergence-tree-prove: the Rishi spoken sweep read something other than converges --\n${rs.out}"

say "GREEN: convergence-tree-prove -- five subjects run twice on a pen checkout, in shell and in Rishi alike, one of them taking two paths rather than a flag, with mode divergence caught, both refusals named, an unperturbed tree refused as proof, a write git ignores refused rather than read as inert, all three pages the pre-commit hook regenerates proven to converge, and a whole-tree sweep proven on the committed tree itself, holding its coupled sayings back on both runs."
